World: stop the MeshAndBsp chunk scan from spinning forever on a malformed mesh.

`World::load` walks the mesh chunk table to find where the BSP tree begins:

	do {
		chunk_type = raw->read_ushort();
		raw->seek(raw->read_uint(), Whence::CUR);
	} while (chunk_type != 0xB060);

Its only exit is finding the mesh END chunk, and neither the length it reads
nor the seek it performs is bounded by what is left in the archive. That would
be recoverable if an out-of-range seek failed, but `ReadMemory::seek`
(`src/Stream.cc`) refuses to move past the end of the buffer and returns
*silently*:

	auto new_position = seek_internal(_m_position, _m_length, off, whence);
	if (new_position > _m_length) return;
	_m_position = new_position;

So a chunk length corrupted large is not a seek to nowhere, it is a no-op: the
cursor stays put, the scan reads whatever follows as the next chunk header, and
walks garbage forwards six bytes at a time. Once the cursor reaches the end of
the archive, reads return zero bytes without advancing it and the seek moves
nothing — `chunk_type` is 0 on every iteration, never 0xB060, and the loop runs
forever at 100% CPU with nothing thrown, nothing logged and no crash.

Measured against our synthetic BinSafe fixture (`test/fixtures/minimal.g2.zen`,
4211 bytes): flipping one byte of the 0xB030 VERTICES chunk length — file
offset 945, the third byte of the little-endian length word — turns 52 into
196660 and hangs `World::load` indefinitely. It was originally found on a real
world with ~500 corrupted bytes, where it ran to 202 s of CPU before being
killed.

The fix is an end-of-file check and nothing else. It cannot change what a valid
world parses: every well-formed world carries its 0xB060 chunk before the end
of the archive, so the check is only ever reached by a file whose mesh section
is already unreadable. It also makes the loop provably terminating — a read
below the end always advances the cursor by at least one byte, and the only
fixed point (cursor at the end) now throws.

`ParserError` derives from `zenkit::Error`, so this reports as a normal parse
failure through the same path a truncated archive already takes.

Upstreamable as-is: a plain robustness bug, no API change, one guard.

diff --git a/src/World.cc b/src/World.cc
index 90c30e6..745f0e9 100644
--- a/src/World.cc
+++ b/src/World.cc
@@ -96,6 +96,20 @@ namespace zenkit {
 				auto mesh_offset = raw->tell();
 
 				do {
+					// The only exit from this scan is finding the mesh END chunk, and the
+					// seek that walks to the next one cannot fail loudly: `ReadMemory::seek`
+					// refuses to move past the end of the buffer and returns silently, so a
+					// chunk length corrupted large leaves the cursor where it was. The scan
+					// then walks garbage until it reaches the end of the archive and spins
+					// there forever — no allocation, no error, 100% CPU. A valid world
+					// always carries its 0xB060 chunk before the end of the archive, so this
+					// check never fires for one.
+					if (raw->eof()) {
+						throw ParserError {"World",
+						                   "MeshAndBsp: reached the end of the archive without a mesh "
+						                   "end chunk (0xB060); the mesh section is malformed"};
+					}
+
 					chunk_type = raw->read_ushort();
 					raw->seek(raw->read_uint(), Whence::CUR);
 				} while (chunk_type != 0xB060);
